---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../lib/links';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Stylebot does with your data: no server, no account, no analytics."
>
  <main class="privacy">
    <div class="privacy-intro">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p>
        Stylebot has no server, no account and no analytics. Your styles stay in
        your browser unless you turn on sync, the agent or the command line, and then
        they go straight to a service you chose or an app you allowed.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">Last updated October 8, 2026</p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What stays in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        Your styles, profiles, settings, version history, chat conversations
        and API keys are saved in your browser's extension storage on this
        computer. Uninstalling Stylebot removes them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stylebot can read and change the pages you visit, since that's how it
        applies your styles. What it reads there is used to style the page, and
        isn't sent anywhere unless you use the agent or the command line.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Google Drive sync</h2>
      <p>
        Sync is off until you connect Google Drive in Options. Your styles,
        including profiles, are then saved to a file in your own Google Drive.
        Stylebot asks only for access to the files it creates, so it can't see
        anything else in your Drive.
      </p>
      <p>
        The access token Google issues is kept in your browser and expires
        after an hour. Disconnect in Options, or remove Stylebot's access in
        your <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">Google account</a>.
        Google's <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">privacy policy</a>{' '}
        applies to what's stored in your Drive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stylebot's use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the{' '}
        <a
          href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
          >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
        >, including the Limited Use requirements.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Stylebot agent</h2>
      <p>
        The agent is off until you add an API key. When you send a message, your
        browser sends it straight to the provider you picked, with your key.
        The request includes your message, any screenshot you attach, the page's
        address and title, an outline of the text and elements visible on the
        page, and the site's CSS and your Stylebot styles for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Page text can include personal information, such as your name or
        messages on the page. Don't use the agent on pages you wouldn't share
        with the provider. Their policy covers what happens next:{' '}
        <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy">Anthropic</a>,{' '}
        <a href="https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy/">OpenAI</a> and{' '}
        <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms">Google</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Command line</h2>
      <p>
        The command line is off until you turn on{' '}
        <q>Let apps on this computer control Stylebot</q> in Options. Stylebot
        then connects to a small program on your computer, and any app running
        as you can use it to list your tabs, read and inspect pages, take
        screenshots, and read and change your styles and profiles. It talks
        through a local socket only you can read, and Stylebot sends nothing
        to a server of its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        What an app does with that is up to the app. Page outlines, screenshots
        and styles it reads can reach whatever service it uses, such as the
        model behind a coding agent, under that service's policy. Turn the
        setting off in Options to disconnect.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Fonts and imported CSS</h2>
      <p>
        When a style uses a Google Font, it's downloaded from Google Fonts, which
        sees your IP address as with any web font. When a style uses{' '}
        <code>@import</code>, Stylebot fetches that stylesheet from its address.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        stylebot.dev has no ads or cookies, and is hosted on GitHub Pages,
        which keeps standard server logs. It counts visits with Plausible,
        which records the page, referrer, country and device type without
        cookies or anything that identifies you. Your theme choice is saved in
        your browser. The feedback form on the uninstall page opens your email
        app, and nothing is sent unless you send that email.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Sharing</h2>
      <p>
        Stylebot doesn't collect, sell or share your data. Data only leaves your
        browser for the services above, when you use the features that need
        them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
      <p>
        Changes to this policy are listed in the{' '}
        <a href={`${LINKS.github}/commits/v4/site/src/pages/privacy.astro`}
          >site's history on GitHub</a
        >. Questions go to <a href={`mailto:${LINKS.feedbackEmail}`}
          >{LINKS.feedbackEmail}</a
        > or a <a href={LINKS.issues}>GitHub issue</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 64px 24px 0;
  }

  .privacy-intro {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 36px;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font: 700 clamp(34px, 4.6vw, 48px) / 1.05 var(--display);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .privacy-intro p {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .privacy-intro .updated {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  section {
    padding: 40px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font: 700 26px/1.2 var(--display);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  p {
    margin: 0;
    font: 400 16px/1.65 var(--ui);
    color: var(--ink2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  code {
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    background: var(--fill);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    font: 400 0.88em/1.5 var(--mono);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .privacy {
      padding-top: 40px;
    }
  }
</style>
